WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.194
Cassini launched in October 1997.

00:00:02.194 --> 00:00:11.967
It took almost seven years to reach Saturn — flybys of Venus, Venus again, Earth, and Jupiter built up the energy needed for the outer-system trip.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:13.562
It arrived in July 2004.

00:00:13.562 --> 00:00:17.618
Cassini spent the next thirteen years in orbit around Saturn.

00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:24.864
It dropped the Huygens probe onto Titan in January 2005 — the first soft landing on a moon of another planet.

00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:31.845
It discovered that Enceladus, Saturn's small icy moon, shoots geysers of water vapor from its south pole.

00:00:31.845 --> 00:00:34.371
Underneath that ice is a liquid ocean.

00:00:34.371 --> 00:00:37.031
Possibly with life-supporting chemistry.

00:00:37.031 --> 00:00:39.756
By 2017, Cassini was running low on fuel.

00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:54.116
And the mission team faced a problem: if they let the spacecraft drift, it might eventually crash into Enceladus or Titan — and contaminate worlds that, by then, were leading candidates for finding life beyond Earth.

00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:56.310
So they planned the Grand Finale.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:07.413
Twenty-two orbits, each one threading the gap between Saturn's cloud tops and the inner edge of its rings — a corridor about two thousand four hundred kilometers wide.

00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:10.072
No spacecraft had ever flown through it.

00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:15.125
Each orbit took the spacecraft closer to the planet's surface than the last.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:24.565
On September 15th, 2017, Cassini fired its thrusters one final time to hold its antenna toward Earth, and dove into Saturn's upper atmosphere.

00:01:24.565 --> 00:01:32.676
It transmitted live data — atmospheric composition, magnetic field, radio signatures of dust — until the radio link broke.

00:01:32.676 --> 00:01:34.139
Then it disintegrated.

00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:36.731
No spacecraft has been at Saturn since.

00:01:36.731 --> 00:01:41.119
The next planned arrival, Dragonfly to Titan, will launch in 2028.

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:44.842
Cassini was twenty years between its launch and its end.

00:01:44.842 --> 00:01:53.352
Most of what you see on Saturn in this orrery — the rings, the moons, the planet's interior structure — is what Cassini learned.

